<pending_commit branch="chromeos-2015.07" change_id="I7eacd0edb2b6ca52b59b74075d17c00b50676d4c" commit="261f12ccdea42e03612e96e301b5512ab856905e" commit_message="vboot: prepare for x86 verstage  In order to support x86 verstage proper the work buffer needs to live in cache-as-ram. However, after cache-as-ram is torn down one still needs the verification results to know which slot was selected. Though the platforms with a dedicated SRAM can just use the work buffer in SRAM, the x86 cache-as-ram platforms need a place to stash the results. For that situation cbmem is employed. This works because when cbmem is initialized cache-as-ram is still enabled. The VBOOT_DYNAMIC_WORK_BUFFER case assumes verified boot doesn't start until after cbmem is up. That doesn't change, but it's a goal to get rid of that option entirely once all other x86 platforms are moved over to pre-romstage vboot.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:44827 BRANCH=None TEST=Built and booted glados with pre-romstage verification      as well as VBOOT_DYNAMIC_WORK_BUFFER case.  Change-Id: I7eacd0edb2b6ca52b59b74075d17c00b50676d4c Signed-off-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/304646 Tested-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Duncan Laurie &lt;dlaurie@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="304646" owner_email="adurbin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" ref="refs/changes/46/304646/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-2015.07" change_id="I097aa0b92f3bb95275205a3fd8b21362c67b97aa" commit="707b820e7c89e5b24415fb4515a0e8a5d5dbf24c" commit_message="x86: add standalone verstage support  To support x86 verstage one needs a working buffer for vboot. That buffer resides in the cache-as-ram region which persists across verstage and romstage. The current assumption is that verstage brings cache-as-ram up and romstage tears cache-as-ram down. The timestamp, cbmem console, and the vboot work buffer are persistent through in both romstage and verstage. The vboot work buffer as well as the cbmem console are permanently destroyed once cache-as-ram is torn down. The timestamp region is migrated. When verstage is enabled the assumption is that _start is the romstage entry point. It's currently expected that the chipset provides the entry point to romstage when verstage is employed. Also, the car_var_*() APIs use direct access when in verstage since its expected verstage does not tear down cache-as-ram. Lastly, supporting files were added to verstage-y such that an x86 verstage will build and link.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:44827 BRANCH=None TEST=Built and booted glados using separate verstage.  Change-Id: I097aa0b92f3bb95275205a3fd8b21362c67b97aa Signed-off-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/303335 Reviewed-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Duncan Laurie &lt;dlaurie@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="303335" owner_email="adurbin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="5" project="chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" ref="refs/changes/35/303335/5" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-2015.07" change_id="I7cd399302a3925a05fbce82600b4c50ea66a0fcb" commit="6e56242b7e66e818b1060a824b61585e889c70d2" commit_message="cbfs: don't load x86 programs over the top of read-only media  On x86 the early stages are currently execute-in-place which means they live in the memory-mapped spi flash. However, when loading romstage from verstage the romstage is execute-in-place so it's unnecessary to write over a read-only meida -- not to mention writing to read-only memory is wrong to begin with.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:44827 BRANCH=None TEST=Built and booted glados. Noted reduction of 20ms when      loading romstage.  Change-Id: I7cd399302a3925a05fbce82600b4c50ea66a0fcb Signed-off-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/304647 Tested-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Duncan Laurie &lt;dlaurie@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="304647" owner_email="adurbin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" ref="refs/changes/47/304647/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-2015.07" change_id="I8de74a41387ac914b03c9da67fd80f8b91e9e7ca" commit="1e79e5a2bab3f7e53d971c601be94ebddde48331" commit_message="fsp1_1: add verstage support  In order to support verstage the cache-as-ram split is taken advantage of such that verstage has the cache-as-ram setup and rosmtage has the cache-as-ram tear down path. The verstage proper just initializes the console and attempts to run romstage which triggers the vboot verification of the firmware. In order to pass the current FSP to use during romstage a global variable in cache-as-ram is populated before returning to the assembly code which tears down cache-as-ram.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:44827 BRANCH=None TEST=Built and booted glados with verstage support as well as      VBOOT_DYNAMIC_WORK_BUFFER with direct link in romstage.  Change-Id: I8de74a41387ac914b03c9da67fd80f8b91e9e7ca Signed-off-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/303336 Tested-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Duncan Laurie &lt;dlaurie@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="303336" owner_email="adurbin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="5" project="chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" ref="refs/changes/36/303336/5" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ica6b18cf3ff54484d1b10f6b08265abed29ac490" commit="097c17250e43c69f35c720d4944e0def3c07a7a0" commit_message="Revert &quot;udev: drop old 208&quot;  This reverts commit f0c80a92e7a735897b4ae83dfe8a8dd5174b4f8a.  We're seeing strange udev failures, specifically:   udevd[1271]: timeout '/bin/chown power:power   /sys//devices/.../power/wakeup'  ...this is leading to a big delay before USB devices (like USB ethernet) are probed.  Failures are seen on at least two different boards running two different kernel versions.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:46029 BUG=chromium:539574 TEST=No more weird udev failures / slow USB probes  Change-Id: Ica6b18cf3ff54484d1b10f6b08265abed29ac490 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/304620 Commit-Ready: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Caesar Wang &lt;wxt@rock-chips.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Mike Frysinger &lt;vapier@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="304620" owner_email="dianders@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" ref="refs/changes/20/304620/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ie7b51416320a3dbc2c79afafe470148a0870c564" commit="0058bb39222227d63499ae42218a705b3d04b448" commit_message="Revert &quot;permission_broker: Poll on fd from new udev_queue_get_fd API.&quot;  This reverts commit 615583164d3b0fa132c7d8dba89bbedb54c1b556.  We're seeing strange udev failures, specifically:   udevd[1271]: timeout '/bin/chown power:power /sys//devices/.../power/wakeup'  ...this is leading to a big delay before USB devices (like USB ethernet) are probed.  Failures are seen on at least two different boards running two different kernel versions.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:46029 BUG=chromium:539574 TEST=No more weird udev failures / slow USB probes  Change-Id: Ie7b51416320a3dbc2c79afafe470148a0870c564 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/304601 Commit-Ready: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Caesar Wang &lt;wxt@rock-chips.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Reilly Grant &lt;reillyg@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Mike Frysinger &lt;vapier@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="304601" owner_email="dianders@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2" ref="refs/changes/01/304601/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I2f526316de36dfdd358fdc46f1b8f373a92e8e0f" commit="6c9d5c38fc7f14a8a9cea7e41546a63e7204226c" commit_message="Revert &quot;udev: unmask 216&quot;  This reverts commit 37bf4968cb62aff2a61f7d31aa091e3f46457ae7.  We're seeing strange udev failures, specifically:   udevd[1271]: timeout '/bin/chown power:power /sys//devices/.../power/wakeup'  ...this is leading to a big delay before USB devices (like USB ethernet) are probed.  Failures are seen on at least two different boards running two different kernel versions.  CQ-DEPEND=CL:304601 BUG=chrome-os-partner:46029 BUG=chromium:539574 TEST=No more weird udev failures / slow USB probes  Change-Id: I2f526316de36dfdd358fdc46f1b8f373a92e8e0f Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/304621 Commit-Ready: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Caesar Wang &lt;wxt@rock-chips.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Richard Barnette &lt;jrbarnette@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Mike Frysinger &lt;vapier@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="304621" owner_email="dianders@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" ref="refs/changes/21/304621/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-2015.07" change_id="I2d0c98760494e2f4657ee35b6f155690939d2d18" commit="1c1ca7211da42b22b6a623b0089276bc2d871e85" commit_message="skylake: add support for verstage  The right files just need to be added to the verstage build. Do that so a stand alone verstage builds and links.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:44827 BRANCH=None TEST=Built and booted glados.  Change-Id: I2d0c98760494e2f4657ee35b6f155690939d2d18 Signed-off-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/304648 Reviewed-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Duncan Laurie &lt;dlaurie@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="304648" owner_email="adurbin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" ref="refs/changes/48/304648/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-2015.07" change_id="Id2b05548e4e10cd12002286913f2228b84802e63" commit="faba1772974357c10ab38c41ead63097fc7c9cdd" commit_message="glados: add chromeos.c to verstage  In order to build stand alone verstage the chromeos.c file needs to be part of the verstage target.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:44827 BRANCH=None TEST=Built and booted glados.  Change-Id: Id2b05548e4e10cd12002286913f2228b84802e63 Signed-off-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/304649 Reviewed-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Duncan Laurie &lt;dlaurie@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="304649" owner_email="adurbin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" ref="refs/changes/49/304649/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-2015.07" change_id="I2b3e088326d5a965ad90806a7950b9f401ed57de" commit="c463e5f7d8312dfd52bae4a48bd75999b95fe6db" commit_message="soc/intel/common: use prog_locate() for finding fsp.bin  The current method was only taking the cbfs path. Because of this fsp.bin was never being utilized from the RW slots. Using prog_locate() now provides both the cbfs and vboot locate methods for free.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:44827 BRANCH=None TEST=Built and booted glados.  Change-Id: I2b3e088326d5a965ad90806a7950b9f401ed57de Signed-off-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/304810 Tested-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Leroy P Leahy &lt;leroy.p.leahy@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Duncan Laurie &lt;dlaurie@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="304810" owner_email="adurbin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" ref="refs/changes/10/304810/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="I0070a7962805482570954c9e6766ad1423bad3f8" commit="cb9898ed278b54ff3ffd3a968d0a12e63fdc9f40" commit_message="drm/nouveau: Make nouveau_gem_do_pushbuf() a kthread  This patch changes nouveau_gem_do_pushbuf() into a kthread so we can have a simple threaded consumer/producer queue model where we can impose sync fence wait with timeout instead of async fence wait on input fences.  BUG=none TEST=Boot to UI and open some apps  Change-Id: I0070a7962805482570954c9e6766ad1423bad3f8 Signed-off-by: Xia Yang &lt;xiay@nvidia.com&gt; Signed-off-by: Tomasz Figa &lt;tfiga@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/303436 " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="303436" owner_email="xiay@nvidia.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/36/303436/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="Iac22a2ddfc04f07c83606a49a2a6225b90a372af" commit="1b50940693cb8e5d7bfe7b7a868ff2c4f4675f2e" commit_message="CHROMIUM: make x86 compilation succeed when CONFIG_ION=y  This is temporary, to unblock other work. There is evidence that this used to work on x86 (at least one shipped device) so we need to figure out where it broke and fix it.  BUG=none TEST=compiles and boots on x86 Signed-off-by: Luigi Semenzato &lt;semenzato@chromium.org&gt;  Change-Id: Iac22a2ddfc04f07c83606a49a2a6225b90a372af Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/304863 Commit-Ready: Luigi Semenzato &lt;semenzato@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Luigi Semenzato &lt;semenzato@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Olof Johansson &lt;olofj@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="304863" owner_email="semenzato@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/63/304863/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I6798f3a4eff67b747845c88a1fc1e759dc6cd678" commit="f0f3088132a9b753a260a613c2fc720b271739d9" commit_message="minijail: make it work correctly with shared mounts  This fixes some problems that appear when system booted with systemd. Systemd sets all mounts to shared. This means that when minijail0 creates mount namespace new mounts will propogate out of that namespace.  This change fixes that by setting all mounts to private right after creating new namespace. Also when remounting /proc it unmounts it lazily, as normal umount() may fail when shared mounts are enabled.  More information about shared mounts: https://www.kernel.org/doc/Documentation/filesystems/sharedsubtree.txt  BUG=chromium:537087 BUG=b:23496617 TEST=security_Minijail0 passes on lakitu_next  Change-Id: I6798f3a4eff67b747845c88a1fc1e759dc6cd678 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/303158 Commit-Ready: Andrey Ulanov &lt;andreyu@google.com&gt; Tested-by: Andrey Ulanov &lt;andreyu@google.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Jorge Lucangeli Obes &lt;jorgelo@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="303158" owner_email="andreyu@google.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2" ref="refs/changes/58/303158/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I067bf5370aaaca2c119107d10a658e69d2272ec9" commit="d906460ca45e03597f93f798c96e84cd9658d702" commit_message="power_RPMTest: fix missing arg for run_test  We were missing the host arg for the test, so this adds it.  BUG=None TEST=run power_RPMTest in the lab  Change-Id: I067bf5370aaaca2c119107d10a658e69d2272ec9 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/305040 Commit-Ready: Derek Basehore &lt;dbasehore@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Derek Basehore &lt;dbasehore@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Todd Broch &lt;tbroch@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="305040" owner_email="dbasehore@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/40/305040/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="Iafa4686789b99ebf40d75c2324de085d1a05a771" commit="acff82850fd78f5cd04f44fface2fa24902a5511" commit_message="UPSTREAM: fs/binfmt_elf.c: fix bug in loading of PIE binaries  With CONFIG_ARCH_BINFMT_ELF_RANDOMIZE_PIE enabled, and a normal top-down address allocation strategy, load_elf_binary() will attempt to map a PIE binary into an address range immediately below mm-&gt;mmap_base.  Unfortunately, load_elf_ binary() does not take account of the need to allocate sufficient space for the entire binary which means that, while the first PT_LOAD segment is mapped below mm-&gt;mmap_base, the subsequent PT_LOAD segment(s) end up being mapped above mm-&gt;mmap_base into the are that is supposed to be the &quot;gap&quot; between the stack and the binary.  Since the size of the &quot;gap&quot; on x86_64 is only guaranteed to be 128MB this means that binaries with large data segments &gt; 128MB can end up mapping part of their data segment over their stack resulting in corruption of the stack (and the data segment once the binary starts to run).  Any PIE binary with a data segment &gt; 128MB is vulnerable to this although address randomization means that the actual gap between the stack and the end of the binary is normally greater than 128MB.  The larger the data segment of the binary the higher the probability of failure.  Fix this by calculating the total size of the binary in the same way as load_elf_interp().  Signed-off-by: Michael Davidson &lt;md@google.com&gt; Cc: Alexander Viro &lt;viro@zeniv.linux.org.uk&gt; Cc: Jiri Kosina &lt;jkosina@suse.cz&gt; Cc: Kees Cook &lt;keescook@chromium.org&gt; Cc: &lt;stable@vger.kernel.org&gt; Signed-off-by: Andrew Morton &lt;akpm@linux-foundation.org&gt; Signed-off-by: Linus Torvalds &lt;torvalds@linux-foundation.org&gt; (cherry picked from commit a87938b2e246b81b4fb713edb371a9fa3c5c3c86)  Signed-off-by: Vincent Palatin &lt;vpalatin@chromium.org&gt;  BUG=b:24042586 TEST=tested along with the &quot;arm64: reduce brk randomization range to 32MB&quot; patch by spawning hundred of thousands of processes, see the details in the other patch.  Change-Id: Iafa4686789b99ebf40d75c2324de085d1a05a771 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/304888 Commit-Ready: Vincent Palatin &lt;vpalatin@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Vincent Palatin &lt;vpalatin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Nick Kralevich &lt;nnk@google.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Kees Cook &lt;keescook@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="304888" owner_email="vpalatin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/88/304888/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><lkgm version="7534.0.0-rc1"/></manifest>